i'm pleased to be joined by the leader  of the legal democrats sir ed davey  sarah thank you so much for being with  us let's just pick up on the weather  conditions that we are facing there are  calls um by tim farren for water bosses  to have their bonuses capped until they  fix leaks is that something that you  would be backing totally i think the  water companies have got away with blue  murder frankly they've been making huge  profits  the bosses have been getting uh massive  bonuses and not only have we not  prepared properly for this drought  seeing pollution in our rivers and lakes  which is killing wildlife so on all  accounts they've been failing our  country  and we need some tough measures on them  so how long would this ban on their  bonuses go on for because some of these  companies are saying they can't get a  handle on the leaks until at least 2050.  we're going to just stop the companies  giving up bonuses up until then i think  we need to review the whole way they  have been operating  and we can do that relatively  quickly over the next year or so  but we've got to tackle the problem  they've been allowing these leaks to  continue for far too long  we've seen a waste of water when we're  desperately in need of water for people  there are drought conditions uh and we  need action and i think tim ferran is  quite right to oppose that so do you  think that this uh supports the argument  then that some are making to bring water  companies back into public ownership  well i think we need tough regulation  and there's just not been that  regulation uh there and we've seen that  allow them to remain private or come  back into public ownership well i think  we can do keep uh the current status but  we need tougher regulations that's what  democrats have argued for a long time  and that's regulations both in terms of  sorting out this water shortage but also  the pollution that we've seen in our  rivers we've been allowing these water  companies to pump their filthy sewage  into our rivers liberal democrats in  parliament have put forward uh draft  laws to stop that and unfortunately  conservative mps have voted against that  so conservatives are allowing the water  companies to get away with their  pollution and their failure to act for  our country let's move on now to the uh  cost of living crisis and the  the huge bills that people are facing  you're calling now for there to be a cap  on the prices and for the price cap not  to rise as it will do on the 26th of  august why are you calling for that  right now  i'm very worried about what millions of  families and pensions are going to face  this  winter  and i've been listening to the  conservative candidates for the next  prime minister for a plan  to tackle this to help people but  they've got no plan whatsoever it's  almost like they're living in a parallel  universe so liberal democrats today are  putting forward  our plan and what we're saying is the  expected rise of 1  400 pounds in the energy price cap on  top of the 700 pounds rise last april  we're saying that new rice should not  happen it should be cancelled and it  should be the government that pays  the suppliers  to make sure that people get the  electricity and gas that they need if we  don't do this we're facing a social  crisis a catastrophe  i think from the evidence we've seen in  the democrats we could  be seeing people going hungry uh not  eating as much  turning off their heating and not  heating their homes so if we're going to  avoid a cold hungry winter for millions  of people we have to have the bold plan  that the democrats are arguing for yeah  we know the danger that people are  facing but i wonder how is this going to  be paid for because this is not going to  be cheap for the government to  essentially step in and control prices  yeah there needs to be a big measure  from the government we're saying there  should be a one-off tax on the oil and  gas companies who are making  tens of billions of pounds in profit  profit they didn't expect to make  because no one expected oil and gas  prices to be as high as they are  and i think it's only fair that we asked  those oil and gas companies to pay  some of their profit  to the government so the government  could then help millions of families and  pensioners that's a fair approach it  would also by the way prevent this  catastrophe which is our country is  heading towards you call it one off tax  so is this the same as the windfall tax  that the chancellor yeah it it it's a  winful test but the chancellor's  approach was very weak uh it didn't  raise anywhere near enough money um it  didn't ensure that um uh some of the  profits they've made over this period uh  faced this tax um and they had lots of  loopholes they uh they were given um we  need a proper uh tax on the profits of  the oil and gas companies we've got a  well thought through proposal and it  would pay the large bulk of this idea of  stopping at the energy price cap rising  in october we've got to help people  i think there's huge anxiety out there  people already  cutting back because of the high price  of energy  now  and with energy prices as high as they  are now the idea that they could go up  by another  400 pounds it's frightening to many  people so we need a plan the democrats  are putting on forward so this is a new  windfall tax so you completely redo the  winful taxes already put in place and  yeah because  you describe them as loopholes but the  the chancellor said that they are  incentives to encourage these companies  to continue investing uh in the energy  supply of this country well before he  put forward his plan when liberal  democrats were arguing for this one-off  tax on oil and gas company profits uh  the chief's active bp said with such a  windfall tax they still continue to  invest let's remember the whole point  about this one-off tax are these are  profits that they didn't expect to make  they weren't in their business plan so  they'll go ahead with investments  regardless of this tax and as you know  there is a growing movement don't pay uk  for people not to pay the energy bills  that are coming on the 1st of october do  you support that idea no i don't but i  want the government to take the bold  measures that liberal democrats are now  arguing for if the government took up  the liberal democrat call for cancelling  the rise in the energy price cap  then there'd be no need for that sort of  campaign and we could deliver the help  that millions of people desperately need  we've seen from the evidence from the  polls  uh many people are already saying  they're  not going to eat as much  many people are saying they're not going  to turn on the heating at all  um that is a frightening prospect i  think we're going to see a social crisis  the like we've never seen so we need a  plan and what has been quite  outrageous frankly is whether it's from  the current prime minister or the two  conservative candidates to be the next  prime minister we've had no plan  whatsoever  the energy price right has been like the  elephant in the room uh they've not been  addressed been addressing it in any  coherent way and we can't have a prime  minister living in a parallel universe  we need people  facing up to the reality that millions  of of our citizens face that's why we  need the plan that liberal democrats are  proposing today so do you support the  idea of the two candidates and the prime  minister sitting down before we get the  price rise in the 26th of august to have  a plan in place yeah i i believe we  should have a recall of parliament i  think it should be emergency budget  and what i'm saying beyond that and no  one else has said this  this is the plan for the budget  so we're saying that emergency budget  which should come as soon as possible  should have as a core of its plan  cancelling the rise in the energy prices  this october

